==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|adūn}}, earlier {{term|of|lang=ang}} {{term|dun|dūne|off the hill|lang=ang}} (compare Latin {{term|ad vallum|lang=la}} > Old French {{term|à val|lang=fro}}, used in the same way).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/əˈdaʊn/|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# {{label|en|archaic}} [[down|Down]], [[downward]]; to or in a lower place.
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtArthr2}}, Bk.X, Ch.xix:
#*: Whan Sir Palomydes had herde hym sey so, he kneled '''adowne''' and asked mercy{{nb...}}.
#* {{w|Edmund Spenser}} (c.1552–1599)
#*: Thrice did she sink '''adown'''.

===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# {{context|archaic|lang=en}} [[down]]
#* J. H. Riddell, ''The Uninhabited House''
#*: I fell from one dream into another; found myself wandering through impossible places; {{...}} peering out into the darkness, to catch a sight of a vague figure standing somewhere in the shadow, and looking, with the sun streaming into my eyes and blinding me, '''adown''' long white roads filled with a multitude of people {{...}}

